Yes. I’d separate the generator from the visible expression rather than treating embarrassment, suffering, loyalty, pride, belonging, and “karma” as interchangeable.

Your genotype/phenotype analogy is useful if we use it loosely:

latent generator
→ ritual / initiation conditions
→ experienced emotion
→ interpretation
→ identity / behavior

The same generator—say costly commitment—can express as embarrassment, pain, sacrifice, secrecy, pride, camaraderie, or resentment depending on the person and context.

That helps explain why initiation rituals can bind people. Several mechanisms can overlap:

costly signal — “I paid the price, therefore membership means something”
effort justification — “I suffered for this, therefore I value it more”
shared ordeal — “we went through this together”
identity transition — old role dies, new role becomes salient
memory salience — emotionally intense beginnings are memorable
scarcity / exclusivity — difficult entry makes membership seem valuable

But those same inputs can produce opposite phenotypes:

shared ordeal → brotherhood
or
shared ordeal → humiliation / resentment / trauma

So covariance is where your idea becomes powerful. Instead of assuming:

embarrassment causes loyalty

you could ask:

Which latent variables co-vary with durable belonging?

Maybe the actual generator is:

voluntary difficulty + shared meaning + fair treatment + successful completion + recognition

and embarrassment is merely one historical implementation.

That gives you a cleaner experimental move:

Initiation Decomposition

ritual
→ cost
→ emotion
→ meaning
→ social recognition
→ identity update
→ long-term attachment

Then ablate pieces. Remove humiliation but keep difficulty. Remove secrecy but keep exclusivity. Keep shared ordeal but add explicit consent. Compare what still produces commitment.

That lets you discover whether humiliation was ever essential—or merely an inherited phenotype riding on top of a deeper generator.

And that’s probably the golden thread here:

don’t preserve the suffering just because it once carried the transformation. Find the transformation, then see whether you can carry it more cleanly.